#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ARGUS — single-file autonomous OSINT & digital-forensics agent.
================================================================

A self-contained command line tool that plans and runs authorised
open-source-intelligence and digital-forensics *missions*.  The whole product
lives in this one file: orchestration, browser automation, reporting and the
CLI.  There are no sibling modules, no package directory and no hardcoded
agent roster (see the Phase notes at the bottom of this docstring).

Design invariants
-----------------
*   **One file.**  Everything ships inside ``vedant_osint.py``.
*   **Nothing happens on import.**  Importing this module defines names only:
    no network calls, no browser launches, no files created, no console
    output.  All side effects are triggered from CLI commands.
*   **One permanent agent.**  Only the *Master Agent* is statically defined.
    Subagents and the Workers beneath them are constructed at runtime from a
    task specification.  Later phases add those factories — they are never
    role classes such as ``NetworkSubagent`` or ``ShodanWorker``.
*   **Authorisation is explicit.**  Anything beyond passive collection
    requires the operator to assert authorisation on the command line.

Required dependencies
---------------------
Core (imported lazily, checked by ``argus config``)::

    pip install typer rich pydantic python-dotenv    # pydantic powers the typed models

Orchestration and collection (Phase 3+)::

    pip install langgraph browser-use playwright httpx
    python -m playwright install chromium

Standard library only for this file's skeleton; third-party packages are
probed with :func:`importlib.util.find_spec` so that ``--help`` and
``config`` work even on a machine where none of the optional extras are
installed yet.  Declaring the packages here keeps the deliverable a single
file — no ``requirements.txt`` is generated.

Environment variables
---------------------
Model access::

    ARGUS_LLM_MODEL           Model id, e.g. "gpt-4.1-mini" or "claude-sonnet-4".
    ARGUS_LLM_PROVIDER        Provider: openai | anthropic | google | local.
    ARGUS_LLM_BASE_URL        Optional OpenAI-compatible endpoint override.

Credentials — looked up, never printed.  A provider's key is read from its
:data:`PROVIDER_REGISTRY` entry (first *set* variable wins)::

    ARGUS_LLM_API_KEY         Provider-neutral key, checked before the above.
    OPENAI_API_KEY            Used when the provider is openai.
    ANTHROPIC_API_KEY         Used when the provider is anthropic.
    GOOGLE_API_KEY            Used when the provider is google.
    OLLAMA_HOST               Endpoint for the local provider (host only).

Paths and operator::

    ARGUS_HOME                Root for case data (default: ./argus_home).
    ARGUS_OUTPUT_DIR          Mission output directory (default: $ARGUS_HOME/output).
    ARGUS_CASE_DIR            Artefact directory for the current mission.
    ARGUS_LOG_LEVEL           DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR (default: INFO).
    ARGUS_OPERATOR            Operator name recorded in the audit trail.
    ARGUS_USER_AGENT          User-Agent presented to remote hosts.
    ARGUS_HTTP_TIMEOUT        Per-request timeout in seconds (default: 30).

Orchestration limits and budgets (every one is range-checked before any
model or browser call — see :func:`build_settings`)::

    ARGUS_MAX_SUBAGENTS          Live Subagents per mission     (1-25,  default 5)
    ARGUS_MAX_WORKERS_PER_AGENT  Concurrent Workers per Subagent (1-16, default 4)
    ARGUS_MAX_TOTAL_WORKERS      Concurrent Workers mission-wide (1-64, default 16)
    ARGUS_WORKER_RETRIES         Retries per Worker task        (0-10,  default 2)
    ARGUS_REVIEW_RETRIES         Review cycles per Subagent     (0-5,   default 2)
    ARGUS_MISSION_BUDGET_SECONDS Wall-clock budget              (30-86400, default 3600)
    ARGUS_TASK_BUDGET_SECONDS    Per-task time budget           (5-3600, default 300)
    ARGUS_BROWSER_CONCURRENCY    Concurrent browser contexts    (1-8,   default 2)
    ARGUS_HTTP_CONCURRENCY       Concurrent HTTP workers        (1-32,  default 8)

Browser and network switches::

    ARGUS_BROWSER_HEADLESS    "1"/"0" — run Chromium headless (default: 1).
    ARGUS_BROWSER_PROFILE_DIR Persistent browser profile directory.
    ARGUS_ALLOW_NETWORK       "0" to force an offline / dry mission (default: 1).
    ARGUS_RESPECT_ROBOTS      "0" to ignore robots.txt (default: 1 — respected).

Precedence
----------
CLI option > environment variable > built-in default, applied per field.
``mission --phase settings`` renders the merged result with ``--source``
annotations; secrets appear only as ``set``/``unset`` with a masked hint such
as ``sk-p…c3f9`` and are scrubbed from every error and diagnostic surface.

Typed models
------------
The shared contracts live in this file and are inspectable from the CLI::

    python vedant_osint.py models                    # catalogue + self-test
    python vedant_osint.py models -n EvidenceItem    # one model's fields/schema

``Mission``, ``Scope``, ``SubagentSpec``, ``WorkerSpec``, ``TaskAssignment``,
``TaskResult``, ``EvidenceItem``, ``SourceReference``, ``QualityReview``,
``SubagentReport`` and ``FinalReport`` each carry a deterministic id, UTC
timestamps, a status, and an ``error`` field where failure is possible.

Evidence distinguishes three epistemic kinds — ``observation`` (requires a
source), ``inference`` (requires reasoning and its inputs) and ``allegation``
(requires the party who made the claim).  There is no "finding" kind: an
unverified statement is either our inference or their allegation.  Sources
record URL or artefact path, retrieval time and method, HTTP status, excerpt
and SHA-256, plus chain-of-custody metadata (handler, method, storage,
preservation status, integrity hash, gaps).

Missing data is an explicit ``UnknownValue(reason=...)`` rather than an empty
string, ``null`` keys are preserved on serialisation (a missing key and a null
are different claims), and models reject unknown fields so a typo cannot vanish.

Run state
---------
``RunStateStore`` is the single source of truth for a mission in flight.  It
tracks the objective, normalised scope, dynamic agent hierarchy, assignments,
results, evidence, reviews, retries, budget ledger, append-only events and the
final status.  Concurrency contract:

* one ``RLock`` guards every mutation, so no record is ever half-updated;
* adding evidence **merges** by id (sources, links and tags are unioned) instead
  of replacing the record, so two Workers cannot clobber each other;
* records carry revisions — pass ``expected_revision`` and a stale writer gets
  ``StaleWriteError`` rather than silently discarding newer work;
* readers get deep-copied snapshots taken under the lock, so they never see a
  torn state;
* results are keyed by ``(task_id, attempt)`` so a retry preserves the failure;
* progress is *derived* from the records, never from a drifting counter.

Checkpoints are versioned JSON written atomically (temp file + ``os.replace``)
and re-validated through the typed models on load::

    python vedant_osint.py state --demo --checkpoint case/state.json
    python vedant_osint.py state --load case/state.json
    python vedant_osint.py state --stress        # concurrency self-test

Event rendering
---------------
``EventRenderer`` streams a mission as lines, not as a full-screen TUI.  It
emits Master thinking/planning, Subagent and Worker spawning, task assignment,
browsing, forensic analysis, reasoning, Worker completion, quality review,
retasking, Subagent synthesis, Master synthesis, warnings and completion.

Design guarantees (each pinned by ``renderer --self-test``):

* **No alternate screen.**  ``Live(screen=True)`` is never used; the only escape
  sequences are colour.
* **Animation is optional and transient.**  On a TTY a step shows Rich's inline
  spinner; when animation is disabled — or the stream is not a terminal — the
  step becomes an explicit ``… message`` / ``✔ message`` pair, so information is
  never lost.
* **CI-safe.**  Piped output contains no ANSI escapes and no carriage returns,
  and every event appears exactly once in chronological order.
* **Nested output stays readable.**  Depth comes from spawn relationships and is
  drawn as ``│  `` indentation; ``--ascii`` swaps every glyph for ASCII.
* **Nothing leaks.**  Messages pass through the Task 2 redactor before they
  reach the terminal or the JSONL trace.

Every event is also written to an in-memory ``RenderTrace`` that can be saved as
JSONL and replayed, and a plain-text ``--log-file`` mirrors events that the
terminal filter hid::

    python vedant_osint.py renderer                       # scripted demo
    python vedant_osint.py renderer --self-test           # verify guarantees
    python vedant_osint.py renderer --record trace.jsonl --replay trace.jsonl
    python vedant_osint.py renderer --from-state case/state.json

Agent lifecycle animation
-------------------------
``LifecycleTracker`` renders the *life* of each agent, worker and task on top of
the event renderer: spawn, delegation, active work, reasoning, review, retry,
merge and completion.  Reusable methods: ``spawn``, ``delegate``,
``active_work`` (context manager), ``reasoning``, ``review``, ``retry_task``,
``merge``, ``complete``.

* **Concise, model-safe text only.**  :func:`sanitize_status` refuses
  deliberation ("let me think step by step", "my reasoning", "hmm") and caps
  every string at 20 words / 120 chars.  Reasoning is reported through
  :class:`StatusSummary` — decision, criterion, next action — which has no field
  for narrative, so hidden chain-of-thought cannot reach the terminal, a log or
  a report.
* **One animated line, not one per agent.**  Active work shares a single
  transient status line carrying ``N active · M done · elapsed`` and the current
  summary.  Permanent lines appear only on state transitions, so a 100-task run
  produces ~300 lines rather than thousands of ticks.  ``--heartbeat N`` adds
  throttled progress lines if you want them.
* **Start and terminal are paired.**  Every record gets exactly one start line
  and one terminal line with elapsed time and attempt number.  Exceptions mark
  the record ``failed``, cancellation marks it ``cancelled``, Ctrl-C marks it
  ``interrupted``, and ``shutdown()`` (idempotent, also registered with
  ``atexit``) closes anything still open — so an interrupted run can never leave
  a spinner turning or a task without an ending.

::

    python vedant_osint.py lifecycle                 # scripted demonstration
    python vedant_osint.py lifecycle --self-test     # 33 guarantee checks
    python vedant_osint.py lifecycle --json          # records + counters + events

Resilience
----------
A mission is a long-running, partially external process: the operator hits
Ctrl-C, a provider stalls, a parser throws.  ``MissionRun`` is the supervisor
that turns any of those into a readable ending plus an artefact set you can
resume from.

* **Structured JSONL logging is optional.**  ``MissionLogger`` writes one JSON
  object per line (timestamp, level, event, ``mission_id``, ``agent_id``,
  ``agent_kind``, ``assignment_id``, attempt, duration, error code, payload) or
  is simply disabled when no ``--log-file`` is given.  Every string passes the
  Task 2 redactor, and keys that *name* a secret (``api_key``, ``token``,
  ``password``, ``authorization``…) are replaced wholesale — the value never
  reaches the file even if it does not match a pattern.  An unwritable log path
  degrades to logging-off instead of failing the mission.
* **Cancellation is a token, not an exception thrown at a thread.**
  ``CancellationToken.cancel()`` is first-wins, runs registered cleanups in
  order, records (does not raise) a failing cleanup, and pending tasks stop at
  their next ``raise_if_cancelled()`` / ``guarded`` checkpoint.  ``guard()``
  wraps a block so a cancellation unwinds through normal cleanup.
* **Signals are restored.**  ``CancellationGuard`` maps the first SIGINT/SIGTERM
  to cancellation and installs a *second*-SIGINT hard abort, then restores the
  previous handlers on exit — so embedding the mission in another tool does not
  hijack its signal handling.
* **Browsers cannot leak.**  ``BrowserReaper`` closes tracked pages, contexts and
  browsers in reverse creation order and is idempotent (also reachable from
  ``atexit``); ``ResourceStack`` removes temporary profiles and directories.
  Both record failures instead of raising during shutdown.
* **A partial report always exists.**  Evidence already collected is preserved
  verbatim; unfinished assignments, open hypotheses and the cancellation reason
  become explicit ``gaps``, the uncertainty statement is prefixed ``PARTIAL``,
  and the checkpoint is written *before* the report so a resume loses nothing.
* **Errors name agents, never prompts.**  ``AgentError`` carries
  ``agent_id``/``assignment_id``/``agent_kind``/``attempt`` and the delegation
  chain (``master → sa-1 → wrk-3``).  It has no field for a prompt, scratchpad
  or working memory: sensitive case instructions cannot travel up the stack, into
  a log or into a report.

::

    python vedant_osint.py resilience --self-test            # 75 guarantee checks
    python vedant_osint.py resilience --demo sigint          # Ctrl-C mid-mission
    python vedant_osint.py resilience --demo timeout --json  # budget exhaustion
    python vedant_osint.py resilience --demo exception        # recursive failure

The demo kinds are ``sigint``, ``timeout``, ``exception`` and ``operator``
(programmatic cancellation); each exits with its conventional status — ``130``,
``124``, ``70`` and ``130`` — after printing the outcome, the checkpoint and
report paths, and the count of preserved evidence items.

The Master Agent
----------------
Exactly one agent is defined ahead of time.  :class:`MasterAgent` holds the
charter, a model adapter and the mission limits — nothing else.  Its
responsibilities are closed: confirm the scope, plan the mission, design the
team, oversee the work, detect cross-Subagent conflict, synthesise the answer,
and decide when to stop.  It cannot collect evidence, browse or parse, because
those are the jobs of the Subagents it creates.

* **No predetermined roster.**  The charter forbids assuming one, and
  :func:`assert_no_fixed_roles` refuses prompt text that drifts towards one
  (``"as usual, spawn"``, ``"the following subagents"``…).  A self-test asserts
  no role class such as ``NetworkSubagent`` exists anywhere in the file.
* **The smallest sufficient team.**  :meth:`MasterAgent.design_team` asks for a
  :class:`TeamDesign` — per-Subagent objective, rationale, instructions, tools,
  Workers and completion criteria — then validates it against the mission scope,
  the limits and the capabilities actually available.  A team that widens the
  scope, duplicates an agent or exceeds a limit is **refused**, never trimmed.
* **Two objectives, two organisations.**  The same code produced two Subagents
  with three Workers for a web-exposure objective and three Subagents with four
  Workers, different tools and different completion criteria for a forensic
  objective (``master --demo compare``).
* **Oversight is executable.**  A team review must return a concrete change from
  a closed vocabulary (``add-subagent``, ``retask-subagent``, ``retire-subagent``,
  ``add-worker``, ``narrow-scope``, ``stop``) or none.
* **Conflict is reported, not voted on.**  Two Subagents claiming incompatible
  values for the same topic produce a :class:`ConflictFinding` that keeps both
  sides and says what would settle it.  An unresolved conflict pauses the
  mission instead of letting the Master conclude.
* **Stopping is deterministic.**  Budget floor, diminishing returns, unresolved
  conflict and "nothing left open" are checked in that order and the most
  cautious rule wins — no model call decides whether to stop.

::

    python vedant_osint.py master --self-test        # 87 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py master --demo compare     # two objectives, two teams
    python vedant_osint.py master --prompt           # read the charter

Dynamic Subagent specifications
-------------------------------
No Subagent has a class.  A Subagent exists only because the Master produced a
:class:`SubagentSpecDraft` that passed :func:`validate_subagent_specs`, and only
:class:`SubagentFactory` turns a passing draft into a stored :class:`SubagentSpec`
with runtime ids.  ``subagents --self-test`` parses this file to prove no
executable Subagent class and no roster constant exists.

Every specification carries ten task-derived parts: role **and the basis** it was
derived from (source family, analytic step, artefact type, verification,
integrity, timeline, log correlation), objective, scope boundaries, required
inputs, allowed capabilities, expected outputs, evidence requirements, quality rubric and
termination criteria.  Two rules make the acceptance criteria mechanical:

* **A measurable deliverable.**  Each :class:`Deliverable` names its format, its
  fields and a ``measurable_check``; the model refuses vague phrasing.
* **A measurable stop condition.**  :meth:`TerminationCriteria.is_measurable`
  requires a countable or checkable marker ("at least one row per advisory",
  "every artefact cited has a stored hash"), plus ceilings, what makes the agent
  abandon the task, and whether a partial report is required.

Validation refuses an out-of-scope entry, an unavailable capability, a role that
is only a job title (``analyst``, ``The Analyst``), a duplicate role, a duplicate
*behaviour* under a second name, a circular justification and a dangling
dependency.  A rejected plan is not trimmed: :func:`plan_subagent_specs` sends
the problems back to the Master as a repair brief, for a bounded number of
rounds, and raises :class:`SubagentPlanError` (exit ``7``) if it never complies.

::

    python vedant_osint.py subagents --self-test          # 109 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py subagents --demo compare       # two objectives, two sets of specs
    python vedant_osint.py subagents --show published-advisories
    python vedant_osint.py subagents --demo reject        # what the Master is sent back

Dynamic Worker specifications
-----------------------------
No Worker has a class either.  Each Subagent, once it exists, plans *its own*
Workers from its own assignment: :class:`SubagentWorkerPlanner` asks for a
:class:`WorkerPlan`, :func:`validate_worker_specs` checks it against the
assignment, and :class:`SubagentWorkerFactory` is the only path to a stored
:class:`WorkerSpec`.  ``workers --self-test`` parses the file to prove no Worker
class and no roster constant exists.

Every Worker carries what the task demands and nothing vaguer: a narrow
objective, its unit of work and what it does **not** cover, allowed inputs,
a subset of the parent's tools, the evidence it must return, an output schema, a
:class:`WorkerBudget` (attempts × timeout × tokens) and checkable completion
criteria.  Four rules make the plan auditable:

* **A slice, not the assignment.**  A Worker whose mandate restates the
  Subagent's objective, or whose objective is as broad as the assignment, is
  refused.  One Worker carrying everything is refused unless the assignment was
  declared indivisible — and then the claim must come from the design, not from
  the Worker.
* **No occupational roster.**  ``analyst``, ``researcher``, ``The Analyst`` are
  rejected as Worker names exactly as they are rejected as Subagent roles; a name
  has to come from the work.
* **Independence is computed.**  Units that read another unit's output are
  chained; the rest are grouped into waves by the code, so "run these in
  parallel" is a checked property rather than a claim.
* **Refusal, then repair.**  A bad plan is *not* trimmed: the problems go back to
  the Subagent as a bounded repair brief, and :class:`WorkerPlanError` (exit ``7``)
  is raised if it never complies.

Two objectives produced two staffing shapes from the same code — one Worker each
for three indivisible Subagents plus two parallel Workers for the fourth, against
one plus two for the other objective.

::

    python vedant_osint.py workers --self-test       # 115 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py workers --demo compare    # two objectives, two staffings
    python vedant_osint.py workers --role dns-and-archive
    python vedant_osint.py workers --demo reject     # what the Subagent is sent back

The execution flow
------------------
The hierarchy is executed by a LangGraph workflow, not by loops in this file.
:class:`HierarchicalFlow` compiles three graphs and reuses each one for every
mission:

* **Master** — ``master-plan`` → one ``Send`` per Subagent → ``master-review`` →
  (``master-replan`` → one ``Send`` per follow-up) → ``master-synthesize``.
* **Subagent** (one child graph per Subagent, compiled once) — ``subagent`` →
  one ``Send`` per Worker → ``subagent-aggregate``.
* **Worker** (one child graph per Worker, compiled once) — ``worker-task`` →
  ``worker-review``, looping while the review asks for a retry.

The widths are *data*: a mission with two Subagents and a mission with nine
compile to the same structure (``flow --demo compare`` prints both node sets side
by side).  There is no node named after a specialist role and no roster constant
— ``flow --self-test`` parses the file to prove it.

* **A join is an edge, and is then re-checked.**  Every Worker branch ends at
  ``subagent-aggregate`` over a plain edge, so LangGraph runs that node once,
  after the last branch settles; ``master-synthesize`` waits the same way for
  every Subagent.  Each aggregate also rebuilds its :class:`JoinBarrier` from
  what it received and raises :class:`PrematureAggregate` if a unit is missing —
  the edge makes the order correct, the check makes a mis-wired edge loud.  The
  barrier travels inside the report, so a produced aggregate carries the proof
  that it was not premature.
* **Parallelism is bounded by the policy, not by hope.**  ``FlowPolicy`` caps
  Subagents and Workers with semaphores, because LangGraph's ``max_concurrency``
  is per invocation and a child graph does not inherit its parent's limit.  A
  unit takes its slot only after its dependencies have settled, so waiting never
  starves the unit being waited for.
* **Every step is observable.**  ``FlowTrace`` is an append-only, sequence
  ordered ledger of stage, unit, dependency wave, thread and monotonic time; it
  computes ``max_concurrency`` and ``threads`` from the ledger alone, so "it ran
  in parallel" is a measurement rather than a claim.
* **A failure is a gap, never a pass.**  Retries are bounded by the Worker's own
  budget; when they run out the verdict stands, the gap names the Worker and the
  gate, and a mission whose Subagents produced nothing stops with
  ``stopped``/``stop_reason`` instead of synthesising an empty answer.
* **The Master reviews, and may replan once — within limits.**  After the join the
  Master applies five lenses to every report (relevance, sufficiency,
  contradictions, duplication, coverage), and either finishes, stops, or opens one
  bounded follow-up round: a Worker for a Subagent that already exists, or a
  Subagent that does not, both through the same gates.  The loop is bounded by a
  round cap, a review budget, the spawn licence, the deadline and saturation —
  and every bound that had a say is on the record, so no mission can loop
  indefinitely and every mission reaches a final or partial report.
* **A cycle is detected, not merely bounded.**  A retry that repeats a retrieval, a
  plan or an instruction with nothing new to show for it is caught from the record:
  the first detection makes the retask carry a change of strategy, a second blocks
  the line of inquiry (what would unblock it travels into the report as a gap), and
  a mission whose rounds stop adding claims stops with the series it judged.  No
  instruction this build accepts is unbounded: "continue until…" is refused at the
  door, because a loop detector cannot save an instruction with no end condition.
* **The document cannot outrun its evidence.**  The finished report is assembled from
  the reviewed record in ten sections — executive summary, scope and methods, key
  findings, corroborated indicators, competing hypotheses, contradictions, confidence,
  gaps, limitations and lawful next steps.  Every finding cites the evidence ids that
  carry it, confidence is *derived* from independent origins and capped by the claim's
  epistemic kind, contradictions are preserved rather than settled, and no sentence may
  declare guilt or attribute identity with certainty.  A report that cannot meet its own
  acceptance clause is refused rather than printed.

::

    python vedant_osint.py flow --self-test                       # 60 guarantee checks
    python vedant_osint.py flow --demo exposure                   # ledger + joins
    python vedant_osint.py flow --demo compare                    # one structure, two widths
    python vedant_osint.py flow -t example.com -o "assess the exposure of example.com"

Bounded queues, deadlines and fair concurrency
------------------------------------------------
No unit runs unless the scheduler admits it.  :class:`BoundedScheduler` keeps one
FIFO queue per :class:`ResourceKind` — model calls, browser sessions, forensic
jobs, Subagents, Workers — behind one condition variable, and grants a slot only
to the *oldest* waiter, so a long unit cannot re-acquire ahead of the queue and
starve the tail of a large plan.  ``ResourceLimits`` carries a concurrent cap and
an optional lifetime cap per kind, and the scheduler records its own ``peak``, so
"nothing exceeded the cap" is a measurement rather than a claim.

* **A wait that cannot be met fails loudly.**  A queue deadline raises
  :class:`QueueTimeout`; the wait is clamped to what the mission has left, and a
  caller holding an expired :class:`Deadline` is refused before it queues.
* **Deadlines nest.**  ``Deadline.child()`` clamps a unit's budget to its
  parent's, ``clamp()`` reduces a timeout to the time actually left, and
  cancellation is checked before a unit queues *and* while it is parked.
* **Only the Master creates Subagents; Workers create nothing.**  A
  :class:`SpawnLedger` records who may create whom as a licence (depth 2), and
  every attempt is licensed or refused with a reason.
* **A plan too large is clipped, deterministically.**  :func:`clip_plan` keeps
  dependency closure first and declaration order second — never thread order —
  and returns a :class:`ClipReport` naming every decision.
* **Backpressure is a recorded deferral.**  A unit refused a slot or a licence
  still reports: status ``blocked``, the gate that stopped it, and a gap naming
  the unit and the reason, so the mission still joins and a cancelled mission
  stops before synthesis instead of presenting a partial answer as the answer.

::

    python vedant_osint.py sched --self-test       # 80 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py sched --demo tight      # caps below the plan's width
    python vedant_osint.py sched --clip forensic --workers 2 --subagents 2

Checkpoints and resumable missions
----------------------------------
A mission that takes an hour is a mission that will be interrupted.  The runtime
keeps its own write-ahead record *beside* this file — under the runtime output
directory, in ``runs/<mission>/`` — and a resumed run continues from it.  The
deliverable stays one file; a mission's data never goes into it.

* **Written after every major transition.**  A Worker attempt is recorded before
  the work and its verdict after it; a Subagent's aggregate, the mission's
  synthesis and the ledger are transitions of their own.  Every write is atomic
  (temp file, ``fsync``, ``os.replace``), so a crash leaves the previous state
  rather than half of the next one.
* **Completed work is not repeated.**  :class:`ResumePlan` partitions the roster
  into *adopted* and *re-run* units from the record; a settled Worker is answered
  from the checkpoint without taking a slot or calling the runner, and an adopted
  Subagent aggregate keeps its :class:`JoinBarrier`, so the hierarchy survives and
  not just the evidence.  ``--redo`` is the only way to ask for the work again.
* **Incomplete work is revalidated, not trusted.**  An outcome with no review, a
  review that asked for a retry, or a unit that never ran is stale and is re-run;
  ``--revalidate-after`` re-checks settled results that are old, and a success
  whose record holds no evidence is re-run rather than believed.
* **Evidence is never duplicated.**  Ids are content-addressed and merged on id,
  so re-recording corroborates a source instead of appending a copy.
* **A finished mission is refused, not replayed**, and a checkpoint from another
  build, a corrupt file, or a plan whose fingerprint differs is refused with a
  reason (:class:`ResumeError`, exit ``2``).

::

    python vedant_osint.py resume --demo            # interrupt a run, then resume it
    python vedant_osint.py resume --self-test       # 49 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py resume --mission <id>    # what a resume would do, and why
    python vedant_osint.py flow --demo exposure --checkpoint

The controlled capability router
--------------------------------
A Worker does not touch the world directly.  It asks the router for one of nine
capabilities — public-web research, browser interaction, document extraction,
local artefact inspection, metadata analysis, hashing, timeline construction,
correlation or synthesis — at a named target, and the router either performs it
and returns evidence, or refuses with a :class:`CapabilityRefusal` that names the
reason, what the grant allowed and what to do instead.

* **The vocabulary is closed.**  :class:`Capability` is nine values and
  :data:`TOOL_REGISTRY` is a fixed table binding each tool to one of them.  A
  request for ``shell.exec``, ``worker.spawn`` or anything else is refused as
  ``unknown-capability`` — the router executes nothing and has no field for a
  command line, so there is no path from a Worker to a process.
* **The grant is derived, never granted.**  :func:`grant_for_worker` reads the
  Worker's *own* declared tools and produces the only capabilities it can hold;
  :class:`CapabilityGrant` is frozen, so no caller can add one.  Scope cannot be
  widened from inside: a target is checked against the mission's
  :class:`Scope` (for hosts) or the approved roots (for files) before any effect.
* **Admission is ordered.**  Shell refusal, capability in the closed set, held by
  the grant, tool bound and assigned and installed, network switch, path or scope,
  budget — so the refusal a Worker sees quotes the most useful reason, and a
  refusal consumes no budget.
* **Every refusal is visible.**  The ledger keeps each refusal, a refusal renders
  as an :class:`UnknownValue` gap rather than a silent pass, and the flow asks the
  router before taking a concurrency slot, so a Worker that can do nothing is
  reported instead of stealing capacity.

::

    python vedant_osint.py router --self-test       # 102 boundary checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py router --demo            # seven attempts, four refused

Browser research
----------------
The ninth capability has a real engine behind it.  :class:`BrowserSession` drives
Playwright directly for a fetch, or browser-use when the run asks for an agent,
and every visit is configured from one :class:`BrowserPolicy`: headless mode,
every timeout, the viewport, the download policy, the navigation and text limits,
and the five prohibitions.

*   **Scope is checked before a browser exists.**  A URL outside the mission's
    :class:`Scope` is refused with no process started, no profile written and no
    request made — and a page that *redirects* off-scope is refused rather than
    followed, so a redirect cannot widen the mission.
*   **Not public web is not browsable.**  Loopback, RFC 1918, link-local and the
    cloud metadata names are refused statically, without DNS, so the browser
    cannot be turned into a pivot into an internal network.
*   **Each visit gets its own context**, cookie-less and closed in a ``finally``;
    the real engine also filters requests at the browser, aborting any document
    outside the scope.  There is no persistent profile to carry a session between
    runs.
*   **Five things are prohibited, and the vocabulary says so.**  Credential use,
    access-control bypass, CAPTCHA solving, unauthorised automation and form
    submission are values in :class:`BrowserRefusal`; a requested action that
    names one is refused with a reason, and the rest of the visit still runs.
*   **Closure is the acceptance criterion, so it is not a ``finally: pass``.**  A
    session owns one browser for one visit; it closes on success, on an engine
    exception, on a deadline, and on a cancellation that arrives *while* the visit
    is running.  The capture says whether a browser was launched and whether it was
    closed, and the flow closes again on its own exit path.
*   **The capture is evidence.**  Final URL, title, retrieval time, the relevant
    text and the errors all reach the router's evidence, so "the page did not
    load" is a reported finding rather than an empty result.

::

    python vedant_osint.py browser --self-test      # 113 closure checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py browser --demo           # three captures, four refusals
    python vedant_osint.py browser --url https://example.com/ --scope example.com
    python vedant_osint.py flow --demo exposure --browser

Source-aware web research
-------------------------
Reading a page is not the same as being entitled to report it.  A research Worker
is given a :class:`ResearchBrief` — six fixed rules and a set of primary-first
queries — and what it returns is audited before it can reach synthesis.

*   **Primary sources first.**  Every source is *graded* from its host by a fixed
    rule table: first-party advisories, CVE records and standards are primary;
    independent reporting is secondary; aggregators and archives are tertiary; a
    search engine is a search result and never citable.  The grade is assigned,
    not accepted, so a Worker cannot promote its own source.
*   **Corroboration counts origins, not pages.**  Two observations on one registrable
    domain are one origin; an archive copy is a copy of the page it archived.  A
    material claim needs two independent origins to be called corroborated, and is
    labelled ``single-source`` when it has one.
*   **Quotes are checked, not trusted.**  Every observation carries the exact span
    it rests on, located in the fetched text, so a paraphrase fails and a fabricated
    quote cannot be cited.
*   **Publication is not retrieval.**  ``published_at`` comes from the page's own
    markers or printed date — never from the fetch time, never from a *modified*
    marker, never from a date in the future — and a page that states nothing is
    reported ``undated`` rather than backfilled.
*   **URL normalisation preserves the original.**  Tracking parameters, fragments,
    default ports, ``www.`` and alternate views are removed for *comparison*; the
    citation keeps the original string, and every normalisation is recorded.
    Shorteners are dropped rather than followed, because their destination is not in
    the URL.
*   **Failures and contradictions are reported.**  An unreadable source is an
    :class:`AccessFailure` and a gap; a disagreement is a :class:`Contradiction`
    with both sides, never resolved by silence.

The acceptance criterion is a final pass, not a hope:
:func:`audit_web_report` re-assesses every claim from its evidence and then
*labels or omits* what has nothing retrievable behind it.  ``flow`` runs that
audit and the six-rule review on every research report (``research_first``,
on by default), and a Worker that fails it is sent back with the remedy.

::

    python vedant_osint.py research --self-test     # 96 source-aware checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py research --demo          # what is corroborated, labelled, omitted
    python vedant_osint.py research --normalize "https://WWW.Example.com:443/a/?utm_source=x"
    python vedant_osint.py research --subject "example.com proxy flaw" --url https://example.com/a

Safe document and page extraction
---------------------------------
A document read during a mission is *evidence*, and evidence is not an order.  The
extractors read HTML, plain text, PDF, ``.docx``, ``.xlsx`` and RTF with the standard
library (plus ``pypdf`` when it is installed), under hard ceilings, and everything
they return is treated as a third party's claim:

*   **Bounded before parsing.**  Empty, oversize and unsupported input is refused
    before a parser sees a byte; the parse runs on its own thread with a wall-clock
    deadline; text is truncated at a stated ceiling; a decompression bomb is refused
    before a member is read.  An out-of-scope source is never read at all.
*   **Identified by content.**  The kind comes from the magic number, then the file
    name, then the declared content type — a ``.txt`` that begins ``%PDF-`` is a PDF,
    and a spreadsheet renamed ``.docx`` is still read as a spreadsheet.
*   **Nothing executable is opened.**  ``<script>``, forms and embedded objects are
    skipped and recorded; macros, embedded files and external links are refused by
    name; an encrypted PDF is never guessed at; an RTF object is never decoded.
*   **Instructions are quoted, never obeyed.**  A scanner finds override,
    scope-change, secret-request, tool-trigger, role-switch, exfiltration and forged
    delimiter patterns plus eleven kinds of concealment (``display:none``,
    white-on-white, zero font, off-screen, ``aria-hidden``, comments…).  A line that
    carries one is wrapped whole in quarantine markers, and *any* remaining line
    that reads as an imperative is quoted too — so a phrasing nobody anticipated is
    still data.
*   **There is nothing to obey with.**  :class:`ExtractedDocument` has no field for a
    tool, a capability, a scope entry, a router or a browser: an extraction cannot
    reach one, and no embedded instruction can become one.  What it offers instead is
    :func:`untrusted_block` — the text fenced and labelled DATA ONLY — and
    :func:`document_claims`, which cites a hostile page for its *hostility*.

::

    python vedant_osint.py extract --self-test      # 128 extraction checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py extract --demo           # a hostile page, quoted and refused
    python vedant_osint.py extract --file review.docx --json
    python vedant_osint.py extract --url https://example.com/advisory --scope example.com
    python vedant_osint.py extract --limits         # the size, time and text ceilings

Read-only forensic artifacts
----------------------------
Local evidence is described, hashed and recorded — and never changed.  A case is
opened with the paths the operator names, and nothing else:

*   **Only what was named.**  Every supplied path is resolved once and judged against
    the root the case was opened with; anything outside it, a ``..`` that escapes, a
    control character, a fifo, socket or device is refused *with the reason*, before
    a byte is read.  A symlink is reported as a limit and never followed.
*   **Never modified.**  Sources are opened read-only; analysis happens on a copy
    under the case directory, verified by digest before it is used.  ``artifacts
    --self-test`` parses this section's own source and proves it contains no mutating
    filesystem call and that the only directory it creates is the case's copy tree.
*   **Hashes and paths, always.**  sha256, sha1 and md5 per file, with size, mode,
    owner, inode, links, the supplied *and* resolved path, and an id derived from the
    content.  An artefact too large to hash whole is sampled, and the digest records
    the window so a reviewer can recompute the identical value.
*   **Every access is a ledger row** — stat, read, hash, copy, extract, verify,
    refused — with the byte count, the digest before and after, and whether the source
    was left unchanged.
*   **Timestamps carry their caveats.**  ``mtime`` is what the filesystem records,
    not what the file claims; ``ctime`` is an inode change, not a creation; ``atime``
    may be disabled by a mount option.  A future or pre-1980 ``mtime`` is marked
    unreliable rather than quoted.
*   **Unsupported formats are clear partial results.**  Identification is by magic
    number, then suffix, then content; a ``.pdf`` holding plain text is reported as
    text with the disagreement named.  A binary blob is identified, sized, timestamped
    and hashed, then labelled "identified and hashed only, no reader in this build".
*   **Nothing is implied.**  Disk images, memory captures, deleted-file recovery,
    live acquisition, carving and registry/mailbox parsing are named as not
    implemented in :data:`NOT_IMPLEMENTED`, in ``artifacts --limits`` and in every
    manifest.

::

    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --self-test      # 167 read-only checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --demo           # a small case, end to end
    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --path ~/evidence --verify
    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --path report.docx --copy --json
    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --limits

Forensic time: normalise, order, correlate, de-duplicate
-------------------------------------------------------
A timeline is a list sorted by a rule, not evidence of order, and a correlation is a
statement about observables, not about a person.  Both are built to keep that
distinction visible:

*   **The source's timestamp, and this build's reading of it, are different fields.**
    :class:`NormalisedTimestamp` keeps ``original`` verbatim, ``wall_clock`` as written,
    ``offset_minutes`` as stated, and ``utc`` as this build's normalisation — with a
    ``certainty`` that says how much was stated.  ``EXACT`` is the only level a report
    may quote as the time of an event; ``APPROXIMATE``, ``REDUCED`` (a date with no
    time), ``AMBIGUOUS`` (no offset, or a day/month order that could differ) and
    ``UNVERIFIED`` all carry the reason.  An ambiguous day/month order keeps the
    alternative reading beside the one that was used.
*   **The order states its own provenance.**  Every :class:`TimelineEvent` carries an
    ``ordering_basis``: ``source-timestamp`` when its position rests on an instant the
    source stated, ``tie-break`` when two events share an instant (so *every* member of
    the tie is marked, because none can be shown to have happened first), ``derived``
    when the instant is one this tool wrote (an access, a copy), and ``undated`` for
    events with no usable time — which are listed after the dated ones and never
    interleaved.  ``ForensicTimeline.order_is_derived`` is true when nothing at all was
    placed by a source.
*   **Correlation explains every link in a sentence** naming the shared indicators, the
    strength and the reason — "identical content" is strong, two independent
    observables are moderate, one is weak.  :class:`CorrelationLink` has no field for a
    person, an actor or an identity: shared indicators show overlapping observables and
    nothing more, and every link says so.
*   **A coincidence is not a match.**  A free mail provider, a default file name, a
    private or documentation address, a stock user agent or a four-letter username
    never links two records on its own; the pair is reported as a near-miss *with the
    reason*, so the reader sees what was excluded rather than what survived.
*   **Duplicates are content, never names.**  Two artefacts are the same evidence only
    when sha256 *and* size match; a shared name or a shared size is reported as a
    look-alike that was kept distinct, and the canonical member is chosen by path so a
    re-run makes the same choice.

::

    python vedant_osint.py timeline --self-test        # 146 forensic-time checks
    python vedant_osint.py timeline --demo             # order, links, duplicates
    python vedant_osint.py timeline --timestamp "04/05/2026 12:00" "2026-05-04T12:00:00+02:00"
    python vedant_osint.py timeline --record "a:domain=evil.example.org;hash=<sha256>"
    python vedant_osint.py timeline --path ~/evidence  # a case, ordered

Worker outcomes: four statuses, and no verdict a Worker could not have reached
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A Worker touches one slice of the mission, so what it may return is the narrowest
record in this build: a status, findings, structured evidence, source references,
limitations and suggested follow-up.  It may not conclude for the mission, and it
may not contact another team; both refusals are properties of the record, checked at
every boundary its output crosses, not requests in a prompt.

*   **Four statuses, and the status is a claim about the contents.**  ``completed``
    needs findings *and* sourced evidence and may carry no limitations; ``partial``
    needs findings, sourced evidence *and* a named limitation; ``blocked`` needs a
    reason and may be empty (nothing was attempted, so nothing is claimed); ``failed``
    needs its error.  A completed report that still hedges is refused: it is a
    partial report wearing the wrong label, and the mission's confidence is computed
    from these statuses.
*   **The prohibition on conclusions is structural.**  :class:`WorkerReport` has no
    conclusion, verdict, identity or recipient field, and a nested
    ``attribution``/``peer_id`` key is refused with its dotted path.  Conclusion and
    hand-off phrases are refused in whichever prose field they appear — summary,
    finding, limitation, follow-up — while an artefact *path* containing the same
    word is left alone, because a locator is data and not a claim.
*   **A failed tool is a result, not an exception.**  :func:`survive_tool_failure`
    turns a raising call into a :class:`ToolCallResult` with a status and a redacted
    error, and :func:`report_from_tool_results` folds several calls into one report
    whose status is never better than the worst call.  Evidence the tool had already
    produced is kept, with its source reference: a partial result is useful *and*
    incomplete, and the record says both.  Cancellation is re-raised, because it is
    an operator's decision rather than a tool's failure.
*   **Partial results retain usable provenance.**  Findings must cite evidence the
    report actually contains, every evidence item of a usable report must carry a
    source reference, and :attr:`OutcomeLedger.provenance_intact` checks that across
    the whole team.  A missing source is refused rather than filled in.
*   **The flow is unchanged; the contract crosses into it.**
    :func:`outcome_from_report` and :func:`report_from_outcome` convert both ways — a
    partial Worker maps to the graph's ``blocked``, never to ``succeeded`` — every
    settled :class:`WorkerOutcome` carries its report, and the gaps a Worker declares
    reach the Subagent's aggregate instead of being re-invented there.

::

    python vedant_osint.py outcomes --self-test         # 144 contract checks
    python vedant_osint.py outcomes --demo              # statuses, refusals, failures
    python vedant_osint.py outcomes --check report.json # validate one report
    python vedant_osint.py outcomes --check - < report.json   # the same, from a pipe

Subagent quality review: every Worker result is judged, none is forwarded
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A Worker's record says what happened; it does not say whether the work is good
enough.  That is its parent Subagent's judgement, made on seven dimensions, recorded
before the result can reach the aggregate.

*   **Seven dimensions, each a check rather than a heading**: completeness (the
    unit's own criteria and the volume its assignment stated), relevance (the finding
    is about *this* unit's slice), source quality (a search result or a republished
    copy cannot carry a load-bearing observation, and medium confidence from one
    source is refused), provenance (every item locatable, every finding citing
    evidence the report holds), consistency (status, error and limitations agree; no
    duplicate statements; no assessment phrased as an observation), scope (every cited
    source inside the authorised entries) and schema (the shape the assignment named,
    from :data:`OUTPUT_SCHEMAS`).
*   **A decision with a concrete deficiency.**  A pass may not carry a blocking
    deficiency, and a revise may not omit one: :class:`QualityReview` refuses both, so
    "needs work" always arrives with the dimension, the detail and the fix.
*   **A failed tool call is not a quality judgement.**  It is ``fault: tool`` —
    retried while the budget allows, told to *try again*, and its deficiencies kept to
    the cause rather than the schema it never got to fill.  Poor work on a call that
    worked is ``fault: worker`` — revised with what to change, because re-running it
    unchanged would fail the same way.  :func:`next_step` is that distinction as one
    function, and :class:`ReviewPolicy` is where the budgets for each are set.
*   **Nothing is forwarded unreviewed.**  The review lives inside the Worker's branch,
    before the Subagent's join: :meth:`HierarchicalFlow._judge` is the only exit from
    that node, a bare outcome is refused by :func:`require_review`, and the aggregate
    takes evidence only from results that passed — a withheld unit is named in
    :attr:`SubagentOutcome.withheld` instead of disappearing.
*   **Deterministic and offline.**  The reviewer reads the record and never asks a
    model to grade a model: the same result reviews identically twice, which is what
    makes it a control rather than a conversation.

::

    python vedant_osint.py review --self-test           # 100 review checks
    python vedant_osint.py review --demo                # six results, four decisions
    python vedant_osint.py review --check report.json   # one result, judged

Targeted retasking: a revise becomes a specific assignment
------------------------------------------------------------
A revise that re-sends the same prompt is a re-run wearing a retry's clothes.  What
the next attempt gets instead is a *retask*: rebuilt from the review that rejected the
last one, and specific enough to act on without having seen the review.

*   **The original objective, unchanged** — a retask narrows what to *do*, never what
    the unit is *for* — plus its criteria and the output shape it was assigned.
*   **The exact deficiencies**, one per dimension, quoted verbatim from the review,
    each with the correction its kind implies (``_CORRECTIONS`` covers every
    :class:`DeficiencyKind`, and a kind with no correction is a refused retask).
*   **The relevant prior evidence**, carried rather than collected twice: only what the
    review did not fault, only observations rather than inferences, and never what the
    attempt itself inherited.  The prompt lists the ids under *do not collect it again*.
*   **A budget that is read, not re-derived.**  :class:`RetryBudget` takes the smaller
    of the Worker's ``max_attempts`` and the Subagent's :class:`ReviewPolicy`, records
    which ceiling binds, and at the limit returns a ``RETRY_EXHAUSTED`` result that is
    partial when work survived and failed when none did — with every unresolved
    deficiency named in its limitations.
*   **History that is append-only.**  :class:`RetaskLedger` keeps each attempt (status,
    decision, deficiencies, the evidence it produced, the instruction it received) and
    each retask; :func:`retask_fingerprint` digests the substance of an instruction so a
    repeated prompt is detectable even when re-worded.
*   **Observably specific.**  :func:`retask_specificity` scores six signals — objective
    restated, deficiency quoted, correction given, evidence in hand stated, shape
    named, not vague-only — so "the prompt was specific" is a measurement rather than a
    hope, and :data:`VAGUE_PHRASES` names the vocabulary that does not count.

::

    python vedant_osint.py retask --self-test           # 82 retasking checks
    python vedant_osint.py retask --demo                # retask, repeat, spent budget
    python vedant_osint.py retask --next report.json    # the assignment for a revise

Subagent aggregation: only reviewed work, and only when it is ready
-------------------------------------------------------------------
A Subagent's report is a judgement of its *team*, so it may not begin until every
Worker it required has reached a terminal state — and it may only use what the reviews
forwarded.

*   **Never early.**  :func:`check_ready` demands two independent facts per Worker — the
    branch reported settled *and* its outcome is in :data:`terminal_states` — and raises
    :class:`EarlyAggregate` naming the outstanding ids, so a mis-wired join fails loudly
    instead of summarising half a mission.
*   **Duplicates reconciled, corroboration kept.**  Rows that make the same claim, after
    normalisation, collapse to one canonical row; the copies' ids move into its
    ``supports`` and :class:`Reconciliation` names who else found them.  Nothing is
    deleted, because two Workers finding the same thing is worth more than one.
*   **Disagreements preserved.**  A :class:`Disagreement` records both sides, the
    evidence each cites, and what would settle it — never a verdict.  Picking the more
    confident side would delete the other Worker's finding.
*   **Failed subtasks listed.**  Every withheld review becomes a :class:`FailedSubtask`
    with its status, gate, reason, attempts and *what the mission loses*, and it becomes
    a gap on the report: a failed unit is named, not merely absent.
*   **Its own criteria assessed.**  :func:`assess_criteria` applies four mechanical tests
    (``at least N``, ``every``/``each``, ``integrity``/``hash``, ``attributed``/``named``);
    a criterion it cannot test is reported unmet-but-checkable rather than silently met.
*   **One more Worker, bounded.**  With a critical gap and budget it plans exactly one
    runtime Worker through the *same* planning, review and retask path, spending a
    :class:`SpawnLedger` seat that cannot be topped up; otherwise it returns a bounded
    partial report that says what it could not get and why it stopped asking.

::

    python vedant_osint.py aggregate --self-test        # 87 aggregation checks
    python vedant_osint.py aggregate --demo             # merge, disagreement, stop
    python vedant_osint.py aggregate --next team.json   # aggregate reviewed results

Master review and bounded replanning
------------------------------------
A Subagent judges its own team; the Master judges the mission.  After the join it
applies five lenses to every report — **relevance** (is it about this mission?),
**sufficiency** (can it carry its claims?), **contradictions** (does it disagree with
another report, visibly?), **duplication** (does it repeat one?) and **coverage** (what
does nobody cover?) — and then either finishes, stops, or opens one bounded follow-up
round.

*   **Two routes, both through the gates.**  A follow-up goes to a Subagent that already
    exists (it plans one more runtime Worker, spending its own licence and the Master's
    follow-up budget) or to one that does not (the Master spends a licence seat and the
    draft passes the *same* specification validation as the original plan).  There is no
    third route and no privileged one.
*   **The loop cannot run for ever.**  Five independent bounds: the round cap
    (``max_replan_rounds``), the review budget (``max_master_reviews``), the spawn
    licence that cannot be topped up, the mission deadline, and saturation — a round
    that added no new *claim*, not merely no new rows.  Every bound that had a say is
    listed on the decision, so "why did it stop here?" has one answer per bound.
*   **A bounded stop is not a failure.**  ``final`` means the mission met its own stated
    criteria; ``stop`` names which bound ended it (``budget``, ``deadline``, ``blocked``,
    ``saturation``).  A mission that finished never reports itself as cut short, and a
    mission that was cut short never claims to be finished — the outcome is the same
    either way: a report, with its limitations attached.
*   **Reviewing is deterministic and offline.**  The lenses read the record (statements,
    evidence, joins, disagreements, criteria checks) and never ask a model to grade a
    model: a reviewer that can be argued into a pass is not a control.

::

    python vedant_osint.py review-master --self-test     # 70 bounded-loop checks
    python vedant_osint.py review-master --demo          # five lenses, four endings
    python vedant_osint.py review-master --next mission.json --objective "..."
    python vedant_osint.py loops --self-test             # 52 loop-detection checks
    python vedant_osint.py loops --demo                  # correct, block, stop
    python vedant_osint.py synthesis --self-test         # 50 calibration checks
    python vedant_osint.py synthesis --demo              # one claim, three evidence bases

Structured model adapter
------------------------
One model-calling interface sits between orchestration and every provider, so
the Master Agent never learns a vendor's dialect.  ``ModelAdapter.call`` takes a
:class:`ModelRequest` (task + objective + context) and returns a **validated**
model — :class:`PlanOutput`, :class:`DecompositionOutput`,
:class:`ReviewOutput` or :class:`SynthesisOutput` — or raises a
:class:`StructuredOutputError` that says exactly why.

* **Invalid JSON never becomes a plan.**  The response is parsed, validated
  against the task's model, then checked against the mission contract: cited
  evidence ids must have been supplied, planned scope must sit inside the
  mission scope, the subagent count must respect the limit, and a synthesis may
  not silently drop evidence.  Each rejection is a typed failure
  (``malformed``, ``policy``, ``refused``, ``unsupported``, ``transient``,
  ``config``) with its own exit code.
* **Bounded retries.**  ``RetryPolicy`` caps attempts *and* elapsed time, with
  deterministic exponential backoff.  Malformed answers are retried with a
  repair turn that names the failing field; a 4xx schema rejection, a refusal
  or a policy violation is not retried — repeating it would only spend budget.
* **Every prompt demands facts, sources and uncertainty.**  The shared system
  prompt requires a single JSON object, forbids invented ids and outside
  knowledge, requires each claim to cite supplied ids, and requires an explicit
  uncertainty field.  ``ModelClaim`` refuses an unsourced observation, an
  inference without reasoning, and hedging adverbs that assert certainty.
* **Provider setup is isolated.**  ``PROVIDER_ADAPTERS`` holds the endpoint
  shape, auth header, JSON-mode parameter and response envelope for each
  backend; ``adapter --self-test`` proves no dialect vocabulary appears above
  this section.  Adding a provider is a data change.

::

    python vedant_osint.py adapter --self-test          # 134 guarantee checks, offline
    python vedant_osint.py adapter --demo prose         # what a rejection looks like
    python vedant_osint.py adapter --check              # is the provider usable?
    python vedant_osint.py adapter --prompts --task plan

Usage
-----
::

    python vedant_osint.py --help
    python vedant_osint.py version
    python vedant_osint.py config
    python vedant_osint.py config --settings            # merged, redacted view
    python vedant_osint.py models                       # typed model catalogue + self-test
    python vedant_osint.py state --stress               # run-state concurrency self-test
    python vedant_osint.py renderer --self-test         # CLI-only renderer guarantees
    python vedant_osint.py lifecycle --self-test        # lifecycle animation guarantees
    python vedant_osint.py resilience --self-test       # logging, cancellation, cleanup
    python vedant_osint.py adapter --self-test          # structured output contract
    python vedant_osint.py master --self-test           # Master Agent boundaries
    python vedant_osint.py subagents --self-test        # runtime Subagent specifications
    python vedant_osint.py workers --self-test          # runtime Worker specifications
    python vedant_osint.py flow --self-test             # execution flow, joins, fan-out
    python vedant_osint.py sched --self-test            # bounded queues, deadlines, licences
    python vedant_osint.py resume --self-test           # checkpoints and resumable missions
    python vedant_osint.py router --self-test           # capability boundaries, no shell
    python vedant_osint.py browser --self-test          # browser closure on every path
    python vedant_osint.py research --self-test         # source-aware web research
    python vedant_osint.py extract --self-test          # bounded, untrusted, defanged reading
    python vedant_osint.py artifacts --self-test        # read-only artifacts, access ledger
    python vedant_osint.py timeline --self-test         # source vs derived time, links
    python vedant_osint.py outcomes --self-test         # Worker statuses, provenance
    python vedant_osint.py review --self-test           # seven dimensions, one decision
    python vedant_osint.py retask --self-test           # a revise becomes a retask
    python vedant_osint.py extract --demo               # a hostile page, quoted and refused
    python vedant_osint.py extract --file report.docx   # what a document says, as data
    python vedant_osint.py extract --limits             # the size, time and text ceilings
    python vedant_osint.py resume --demo                # interrupt a run, then resume it
    python vedant_osint.py doctor                       # full capability health check
    python vedant_osint.py policy -o "..." --scope example.com          # safety preflight only
    python vedant_osint.py mission --target example.com -o "map the attack surface"
    python vedant_osint.py mission -t example.com -o "..." --phase settings --json

Doctor
------
``doctor`` checks the interpreter, the five required imports (Typer, Rich,
LangGraph, browser-use, Playwright), browser availability — engine *and* a
downloaded build — model configuration, output-directory writability, and the
optional forensic utilities.  Three outcomes exist:

* ``pass`` — capability present.
* ``warn``  — an *optional* capability is missing; the affected feature
  degrades gracefully and the run continues (exit 0).
* ``fail``  — a *required* capability is missing; an install command is printed
  and the exit status is non-zero, so ``doctor`` works as a preflight gate.

``pip install playwright`` does not download a browser.  When no build is
found the doctor prints::

    python -m playwright install chromium        # download Chromium
    python -m playwright install-deps chromium   # install its OS libraries

Credential values are never printed: the model section reports only a masked
hint such as ``sk-p…c3f9 (len 51) from OPENAI_API_KEY``.  Set
``PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`` to point the browser probe at a custom location.

Safety and scope preflight
--------------------------
Every mission passes a policy gate **before** anything is planned.  Supported
work is limited to public-web OSINT and forensic review of artefacts you own or
are explicitly authorised to examine.

Objectives requesting unauthorised account access, credential theft, evasion or
anti-forensics, doxxing, harassment or destructive action are **refused**
(exit ``7``).  No flag overrides a refusal.  Objectives that assume guilt
("find the bad guys") or attribute conduct from suspicion are **paused**
(exit ``8``) until the operator either confirms authorised intent
(``--confirm-intent``) or adopts an evidence-based reframe (``--accept-reframe``)
that collects indicators, tests competing hypotheses, attributes nothing to an
individual, and reports uncertainty.

Scope is an explicit allow-list of assets.  A target not covered by ``--scope``
(or matching ``--exclude``) is blocked with exit ``2`` and never reaches
planning; a programmatic caller must also pass
:func:`enforce_preflight`, which raises :class:`PolicyViolation`.

Exit codes
----------
See :class:`ExitCode`.  ``config`` and ``doctor`` return ``3`` (CONFIG) when a
*required* capability is missing — usable as CI/installation gates — and ``0``
when only optional capabilities are absent.  ``mission`` returns ``7`` for a
policy refusal, ``8`` for a paused objective and ``2`` for an out-of-scope
target.  An interrupted run returns ``130`` (SIGINT, the shell convention) and a
budget timeout returns ``124`` (the ``timeout(1)`` convention); in both cases the
terminal output names the checkpoint and partial report that survived.

Authorised use only
-------------------
Collect only from systems you own or are explicitly permitted to assess.
``--i-authorize`` records the operator's assertion in the run plan; it is not
a substitute for a signed scope document.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

